id: pm-2htk4p
title: "GH-976: version history hashes so repeated tests_add streams verify across supported pm versions"
description: "Track GitHub issue #976: identical history streams with two or more tests_add events verify under pm 2026.8.10 but drift under 2026.8.7, and history-repair alternates which reader accepts the stream instead of converging."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[4]: compatibility,format-version,github,history-integrity
created_at: "2026-08-11T08:34:09.403Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-11T16:29:52.249Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-11T16:21:49.080Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-11T16:21:49.080Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 240
acceptance_criteria: History/item hash epochs are explicit and backward-safe; two-plus tests_add cross-version fixtures never report false corruption; repair refuses incompatible epochs or converges; strict validation distinguishes format incompatibility from drift
parent: pm-4jk8kx
risk: high
confidence: high
severity: high
environment: global pm 2026.8.10 with repository-local @unbrained/pm-cli 2026.8.7
repro_steps: "Append two tests_add operations to one item with 2026.8.10, compare health/strict history drift under 2026.8.10 and 2026.8.7, then run history-repair under each version."
resolution: "Introduced explicit item-hash epochs with legacy auto-detection, order-preserving epoch 2 writes, epoch-aware drift caching, and fail-closed unknown-version repair."
expected_result: Supported readers either verify the same immutable stream or report a precise unsupported format epoch; repair converges to one canonical representation.
actual_result: "Current readers validate isolated history streams produced by published 2026.8.7 and 2026.8.10 after repeated test additions; new events declare epoch 2, supported legacy streams auto-detect, and unsupported explicit epochs refuse precise repair."
component: history hashing and format compatibility
customer_impact: "Pinned CI and global developer pm can permanently disagree, blocking releases and encouraging destructive repair loops."
